{
  "schema": "n39-author-method-assumptions@1",
  "status": "selected_visible_conditions_not_source_edits_or_historical_approval",
  "table_identity": "V. M. Bradis, Four-figure Mathematical Tables, 13th stereotype edition, Drofa, Moscow, 2010. PDF SHA256 249b865dc7ae985ecfa6604531efb5e8b7896c481db1a5e2f4170940f4033656. Historical equivalence to the original textbook's tables is not established.",
  "cells": "Literal source capture records only; decimal commas may be normalized to decimal points in the supplementary view table. Printed trailing zeros are retained. Main row/column and correction-column addresses remain visible.",
  "IV_lookup": "Below 10, a row N plus main-column digit times 0.01 identifies a main radicand; at or above 10 the increment is 0.1. Add the fourth-digit correction in units of 0.001 to the lower main root. This selected additive route is supported by IV examples, including fourth digits above 5, and by the current lesson. It is not the mandatory next-main subtraction rule of III.",
  "806d": "For this new view, explicitly round each radicand to four significant figures first: 1.0058 -> 1.006; 6.83251 -> 6.833; 20.156 -> 20.16. This is a selected preprocessing method, not an asserted universal Bradis rule or the unique historical solution. Extended corrections exist but a complete six-significant-digit route is not provided by these cells.",
  "811": "Look up both components, apply decimal scaling, retain all digits of the resulting table numbers in addition/subtraction, then round the arithmetic total to four significant figures. This is an explicitly selected final display convention; the source itself requests computation but no final digit count.",
  "812": "Use literal table roots 1.414 and 1.732 for the first method and 2.449 for sqrt(6). The extra literal main cell sqrt(3) is absent from the authorized captures; obtain sqrt(3) only by the exact identity sqrt(3)=sqrt(75)/5 using the captured main value 8.660. This yields 1.732 from an actual table value, not a fabricated cell.",
  "813": "Simplify the exact products under a single radical, use the additive IV lookup, and round the resulting table number to 0.01. At a tie in the table number, use nearest with half-up for positive values. In particular 3.735 -> 3.74. This explicit final convention is not proved to be a printed historical tie policy. Direct high-precision rounding of sqrt(13.95) instead gives 3.73; it is not substituted for the specified table route.",
  "814": "First obtain the two literal main roots and multiply those rounded numbers without further rounding, then separately find the exact symbolic product. 5.999602 is the arithmetic product of table approximations, not a claim of seven reliable significant figures; a four-significant-figure display would be 6.000.",
  "815": "Follow the source hint: preserve the approximate radicand's significant-figure count in the root, excluding leading zeros. Retain trailing zeros that convey this count. No guaranteed uncertainty interval follows.",
  "816": "Selected table-first method: use Table III main cells at four significant figures as one reserve digit for three-significant-figure approximate inputs; scale the squares; add/subtract without further intermediate rounding; round the resulting positive radicand to four significant figures to fit the supplied IV grid; use additive IV lookup; finally retain three significant figures. This full staging is visible, not falsely described as uniquely mandated by the historical source. No accuracy guarantee beyond the heuristic precision convention is asserted.",
  "817_818": "Variables are real. Strict inequalities exclude every zero factor, including squared factors. A real radical requires a nonnegative radicand, and an entire denominator must be nonzero.",
  "819": "The written domain [-6,8] is exact. Unlabelled crossings and broad turning points are readings, not exact coordinates. Name the three actual crossings alpha<beta<gamma and the two turning abscissae p<q. Give exact sign/monotonic set descriptions in those symbols, with numerical readings alpha about -4, beta about 0.6, gamma about 3; p about -2, q about 2. Include finite-domain endpoints where applicable. Range is exactly [f(-6),f(8)] for the displayed curve, approximately [-1.5,3]."
}
